  <h1>What Is a Cut-off?</h1>
  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> A cut-off is the analyte concentration threshold that separates a positive (reactive) result from a negative (non-reactive) result. Samples at or above the cut-off read positive; below it, negative.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The test is designed so the visible line appears only when the analyte concentration reaches the cut-off.</li>
    <li>Example: a THC urine test with a 50 ng/mL cut-off is positive only when THC metabolite is ≥ 50 ng/mL.</li>
    <li>Cut-offs are set per analyte and align with regulatory standards (e.g., SAMHSA-style workplace thresholds).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>The cut-off determines both false-positive and false-negative risk. A lower cut-off catches more true cases (higher sensitivity) but more false positives; a higher cut-off does the reverse. Buyers must confirm the cut-off matches their intended use (screening vs confirmation).</p>

